import Mathlib

/-!
# MathDB #375936 — binary Hankel monotonicity is FALSE

Write-up `mathdb-open-problems/problems/375936/solution.tex`,
*Infinitely many failures of binary Hankel monotonicity*, 18 September 2026.

With `s a` the number of ones in the binary expansion of `a`, set
`H n = det [s (i+j)]_{0 ≤ i,j < n}` and `ν k = ⌈2^(k+2)/3⌉`.  Sobolewski–Ulas Conjecture 4.7
asserts that `|H|` is strictly increasing on `[ν k, 2^(k+1)+1]` **and** strictly decreasing
on `[2^(k+1)+1, ν (k+1)]`, for every `k ≥ 0`.

`H 25 = -738` and `H 26 = 351`, and `25 < 26` both lie in `[ν 4, 33] = [22, 33]`, so
`|H 25| = 738 > 351 = |H 26|` and the conjecture fails.

## The 215 point: one violation, not two

The write-up refutes **both** halves (it also gives `H 39 = -432`, `H 40 = -927`).  Refuting
a single half is a *stronger* claim than refuting the conjecture, so the weakest reading the
witness still kills needs only the `25, 26` pair — and this file therefore never has to reach
a 40 × 40 determinant.  The write-up also notes the values are tabulated in OEIS A056886 and
does not claim their discovery; neither do we.

## Why there is a PLU certificate here

`Matrix.det` is a sum over `n!` permutations, so for `n = 25` neither `decide` nor cofactor
expansion can evaluate it.  Instead the file exhibits `L` and `U` with `A.submatrix (swap 0
1) id = L * U`, checked by `decide`, and reads the determinant off `det_permute`, `det_mul`
and the two triangular-determinant lemmas.  Pivoting is forced, not cosmetic: `A 0 0 = s 0 =
0`.  The factors were computed and the identity `L * U = permuted A` verified in exact
rational arithmetic by `Campaigns/MathDB-Formalization/gen_375936.py`, which emits this file;
their entries are at most 7–8 digits.

The connection between the explicit matrices and `s` is the LAST declaration in the file, so
that everything above it is still measured if kernel evaluation of `Nat.digits` disappoints.
-/

